WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.020
Go ahead and play the lottery, whatever. But just&nbsp;
let's remember what it is. It's a tax on the poor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.020 --> 00:00:08.040
When you buy a lottery ticket, all of that money&nbsp;
goes into a big pot. And in my mind it's like, oh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:10.800
it goes into this big pot, and then whoever&nbsp;
wins it gets all that money. That's not how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:14.820
it works. Only like 40% of it goes to the&nbsp;
actual, like, winner of the jackpot. A lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:19.200
of the other money goes to the stores that&nbsp;
sell the lottery tickets. The company that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:24.600
like, helps advertise and run the lottery. And of&nbsp;
course, a giant chunk goes to Uncle Sam. I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:27.660
that's why lotteries exist. That's why&nbsp;
governments run the lottery is because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:32.040
they need to raise extra money. People don't&nbsp;
like taxes, so the lottery just makes taxes fun.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:36.240
That money usually goes into public programs like&nbsp;
parks and education. Or in the case of Nebraska,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:42.180
it goes into like funding their state fair. OK.&nbsp;
The fact is the lottery is a tax and it is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.180 --> 00:00:47.940
tax that's borne particularly by people who have&nbsp;
less education, who live in poorer neighborhoods,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:52.980
and whose money goes into the big lottery&nbsp;
jackpot and doesn't actually come back to benefit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:57.480
their neighborhood. It's not like a tax that&nbsp;
actually benefits them in any material way. So.

